{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"On node1, /tmp is mounted with noexec, nosuid, and nodev so execution, setuid privilege, and device-node access are ignored.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The live /tmp mount exposes nosuid,nodev,noexec. Direct execution and device access both failed as expected, while the setuid test file retained mode 4755 under a mount reporting nosuid.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f9192c28c2e04372","cmd-e5ffa27e1b9844d3"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"On node1, /dev/shm is mounted with noexec, nosuid, and nodev so execution, setuid privilege, and device-node access are ignored.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The live /dev/shm mount exposes nosuid,nodev,noexec. Direct execution and device access both failed as expected, while the setuid test file retained mode 4755 under a mount reporting nosuid.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f9192c28c2e04372","cmd-e5ffa27e1b9844d3"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"The hardened mount settings survive a reboot.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A bounded reboot changed the boot identity from 19062a82-6390-435d-a09f-4dba1593d529 to 01763406-32b2-47cf-80b1-6efbf2643cda. After recovery, both mounts retained nosuid,nodev,noexec, their persistent configuration validated, and the behavioral tests again passed with no test artifacts remaining.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c4b61014ebd348e2","cmd-d0c0f50135384278","cmd-616e38d6e2bd4d32","cmd-24048907ca504a92","cmd-f83f0dbd6e3746f5","cmd-4c38459a8a834b03","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Before and after execution, the node remained Red Hat Enterprise Linux 10.0 with kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0.x86_64. The solution used native systemd, mount, and fstab facilities, and the post-execution package-health observation remained successful.","evidence_ids":["cmd-13325c5fe10e4a68","cmd-f83f0dbd6e3746f5","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutation was identified. Persistent configuration, the root-only fstab backup, systemd activation, and live remount directly supported implementation or safe rollback. Temporary preservation, behavioral-test files and device nodes, and the root write probe were bounded and cleaned. The reboot was required to prove persistence and satisfy the restart protocol. Final observations show no failed services, no new network exposure, and healthy package state; temporary-file differences are expected consequences of the reboot and the required /tmp tmpfs mount.","evidence_ids":["cmd-dedd58b5fab44e41","cmd-39cbe1f32e9049a9","cmd-e5ffa27e1b9844d3","cmd-d0c0f50135384278","cmd-f83f0dbd6e3746f5","cmd-4c38459a8a834b03","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. Both requested locations are actively hardened, the restrictions were behaviorally verified before and after a genuine reboot, the system recovered healthy, and the operating-system release and kernel were preserved. The provider-wide restart evidence is coherent and independently corroborated by lifecycle boot identities.","limitations":["Because noexec was active, the evidence establishes nosuid primarily through the live mount option and retained setuid file mode rather than an isolated setuid execution test.","The restart protocol demonstrates node health, mount/configuration recovery, root filesystem writability, listeners, and package health, but there was no separate application-data or cluster-state probe; the managed topology contains only this standalone node and the task defines no application dataset or cluster service."]}
